# What an FCPM machine prefers. One line per language: <language> <tool>.
# Use these when scaffolding or adding a dependency, unless the project already
# diverges — then say so in THAT project's README, never by editing this file.
#
# A DECISION, NOT AN OBSERVATION. Reality is what is on the box. station-node
# keeps the same two things in two files for exactly this reason: mixing them
# means the decision churns every time the disk changes.
#
# WHY THIS IS SHORTER THAN IT LOOKS LIKE IT SHOULD BE. Most of this repository's
# work is not a language's work. Ruby is here because Jekyll is, and Python is
# here because every script in `bin/` and `site/bin/` is standard-library
# Python 3 run directly — there is no dependency to install and therefore no
# tool to prefer, which is why `python` names the interpreter rather than a
# package manager. Node appears only in `worker/` and `site/tests/`.
#
# WHAT WINDOWS CHANGED. On macOS a preference like `python uv` is complete,
# because how the tool arrives is somebody else's file — brew, asdf, a version
# manager. On Windows the tool and the way it arrives are the same act:
# `winget install` is how both a language and its package manager appear, so
# the preference and the provisioning stop being separable. Autumn, 2026-09-23:
# *"On Windows, I'm using WinGet in the toolchain, which means that toolchain
# as a file is no longer like that simple over in the station node."*
#
# So the third column is the divergence: HOW IT ARRIVES. It is not in
# station-node's version of this file and it has to be in ours, because on a
# fresh Windows box nothing below is present until somebody names the package.
# Where the column is empty, nobody has established it yet.

# language   tool        arrives by
ruby         bundler     # unestablished — see `machines/kiosk-1/PROFILE.md`
python       python3     # unestablished; the winget id is probably Python.Python.3.x
node         npm         # unestablished; `worker/` and `site/tests/` both use npm ci
